The chart is divided into four quadrants, with each quadrant having a representation of Low, Medium, and High risks. I want the y scale to be Low/Medium/High and the angle to represent the proportion of all risks at each level.

A picture is worth a thousand words:

DATA <- data.frame(Quadrant = factor(rep(c("First", "Second", "Third", "Fourth"), each = 3), levels = c("", "First", "Second", "Third", "Fourth")),
                   X = c(1/6, 3/6, 5/6, 7/6, 9/6, 11/6, 13/6, 15/6, 17/6, 19/6, 21/6, 23/6),
                   Risk = factor(rep(c("Low", "Medium", "High"), times = 4), levels = c("", "Low", "Medium", "High")),
                   Count = c(7/10, 2/10, 1/10, 6/10, 3/10, 1/10, 8/10, 2/10, 0, 7/10, 3/10, 0)
)

ggplot(DATA, aes(x = X, y = Risk)) +
  geom_bar(stat = "identity") +
  scale_x_continuous(labels = levels(DATA$Quadrant)) +
  coord_polar(theta = "x")

This produces:

 <https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/-J_qRJergAU4/VK0eY_8wrEI/AAAAAAAACKo/v9BfKUWkllQ/s1600/Rplot01.png>

What is wrong with this plot is that the bars all have the same angle. I want the angle subtended by the bar to represent Count (which I have converted to a proportion for simplicity).
In order to achieve this I would need to use theta = "y" and set the y aesthetic to come from Count, but this would completely change the axes so that I would lose my quadrants!

Any advice appreciated!

I'm thinking that I would need to produce a number of plots independently and then overlay them on top of each other...

