Just thinking out loud...

The first thing I have discovered is that geom_bar() is not the appropriate geom to use in this instance. I need to use geom_rect().

DATA <- data.frame(Xmin = c(0L, 0L, 0L),
                   Xmax = factor(c("High","Medium","Low"), levels = c("Low", "Medium", "High")),
                   Ymin = c(0, 0.1, 0.3),
                   Ymax = c(0.1, 0.3, 1)
                   )

ggplot(DATA, aes(xmin = Xmin, xmax = Xmax, ymin = Ymin, ymax = Ymax, fill = Xmax)) +
  geom_rect() +
  scale_x_discrete(labels = c("Low", "Medium", "High")) +
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, 4), breaks = 0:4, labels = c("First", "Second", "Third", "Fourth", "")) +
  scale_fill_manual(name = "Risk level", values = c("darkgreen", "orange", "red")) +
  coord_polar(theta = "y")

This produces:

 <https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-ZaPr38Swnho/VK3W3LPd4oI/AAAAAAAACLw/d9NxXGAv8Zs/s1600/Rplot.png>

The obvious thing that I have noticed is that the areas of the segments appear roughly the same - despite the fact that the angles are 0.1, 0.2, and 0.7, respectively. I might have to resort to using a geom_line() where the length of the line represents the proportion of risk and the colour scale will represent the risk level.

